00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:13.850
it's not one
person's job anymore.

00:15:13.850 --> 00:15:16.010
You need a team.

00:15:16.010 --> 00:15:18.750
Some decisions are
best centralized,

00:15:18.750 --> 00:15:21.050
like purchasing
committed use discounts,

00:15:21.050 --> 00:15:23.700
or standardizing cost
allocation strategy.

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:27.830
Whereas others are more
effective when decentralized,

00:15:27.830 --> 00:15:31.310
like re-factoring your
application and right sizing.

00:15:31.310 --> 00:15:33.470
Because the
application owner knows

00:15:33.470 --> 00:15:37.490
what's best given their
current architecture.

00:15:37.490 --> 00:15:39.770
No matter what you
decide to improve,

00:15:39.770 --> 00:15:43.660
always define metric,
set target goals,

00:15:43.660 --> 00:15:47.370
and measure often to
track your progress.

00:15:47.370 --> 00:15:51.870
For example, for my application,
Meme Generator 2000,

00:15:51.870 --> 00:15:55.290
I want committed use discount
coverage and utilization

00:15:55.290 --> 00:15:57.750
to be above 90%.

00:15:57.750 --> 00:16:00.100
That's my target.

00:16:00.100 --> 00:16:03.280
Avoid going from 0 to hero.

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:04.900
Start slow.

00:16:04.900 --> 00:16:08.380
Get real-time feedback
and [INAUDIBLE] overtime.

00:16:08.380 --> 00:16:10.940
In industry standard
terms, that's

00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:15.540
learning to crawl and walk
first before you drive.

00:16:15.540 --> 00:16:17.800
I mentioned real-time
feedback, which

00:16:17.800 --> 00:16:20.410
is very critical
element for creating

00:16:20.410 --> 00:16:23.980
a cost conscious culture,
as it can help you course

00:16:23.980 --> 00:16:26.140
correct sooner.

00:16:26.140 --> 00:16:29.020
Think of Prius effect.

00:16:29.020 --> 00:16:31.900
It drives better behaviors.

00:16:31.900 --> 00:16:34.080
We have also seen
customers gamify

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:37.610
the impact, which gets everyone
excited in greeting a shared

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:39.430
goal.

00:16:39.430 --> 00:16:40.670
A quick look.

00:16:40.670 --> 00:16:43.560
Check out our white
paper and other assets

00:16:43.560 --> 00:16:46.450
for comprehensive view
on cost optimization.

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:54.600
Here are some quick wins we have
seen across multiple customers

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:58.710
when helping them over
the period of last year.

00:16:58.710 --> 00:17:00.240
Now, I would like
to hand it over

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:04.089
to Scott from OpenX for
their transformative journey.

00:17:07.050 --> 00:17:09.050
SCOTT SNELL: Thank you Pathik.

00:17:09.050 --> 00:17:10.970
Hi, my name is Scott Snell.

00:17:10.970 --> 00:17:15.220
And I've been working with OpenX
on our Google Cloud migration.

00:17:15.220 --> 00:17:18.690
OpenX is a leader and
programmatic advertising,

00:17:18.690 --> 00:17:22.700
and we operate one of the
world's largest ad exchanges.

00:17:22.700 --> 00:17:25.280
We reach nearly a
billion consumers,

00:17:25.280 --> 00:17:27.710
and we work with more
than 30,000 advertisers

00:17:27.710 --> 00:17:30.910
across every screen and device.

00:17:30.910 --> 00:17:34.120
My focus has been on Cloud
cost management and cost

00:17:34.120 --> 00:17:36.180
optimization.

00:17:36.180 --> 00:17:37.840
Thank you all for joining.

00:17:37.840 --> 00:17:40.990
I'm excited to share some of
the cost optimization learnings

00:17:40.990 --> 00:17:41.490
from OpenX.

00:17:45.280 --> 00:17:47.140
Here is our agenda.

00:17:47.140 --> 00:17:49.220
I'll briefly cover the
challenges for OpenX

00:17:49.220 --> 00:17:51.370
in moving to GCP.

00:17:51.370 --> 00:17:53.020
I'll share how we
prioritized things

00:17:53.020 --> 00:17:57.500
early on from a governance and
cost management perspective.

00:17:57.500 --> 00:18:01.400
Next, I'll cover our approach
to cost visibility and share

00:18:01.400 --> 00:18:03.680
a number of examples of how
we were able to implement

00:18:03.680 --> 00:18:06.640
various cost optimizations.

00:18:06.640 --> 00:18:08.950
We'll finish up with a
review of lessons learned.

00:18:13.340 --> 00:18:15.460
So the challenge
was how to migrate

00:18:15.460 --> 00:18:18.310
15 million lines of
code from six data

00:18:18.310 --> 00:18:21.940
centers worldwide in six months.

00:18:21.940 --> 00:18:25.500
OpenX consumes a significant
amount of compute, network,

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:27.980
and storage resources.

00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:31.960
And we process trillions
of events per day.

00:18:31.960 --> 00:18:35.230
In terms of migrating to
GCP, the engineering teams

00:18:35.230 --> 00:18:38.910
did all of the heavy lifting
and did a fantastic job.

00:18:38.910 --> 00:18:41.500
And the migration was a success.

00:18:41.500 --> 00:18:43.570
But early on in the
migration effort,

00:18:43.570 --> 00:18:47.138
we needed to organize how we
were going to get started.

00:18:47.138 --> 00:18:48.180
So let's talk about that.

00:18:51.510 --> 00:18:55.530
At the beginning of the
project, Paul Ryan, our CTO,

00:18:55.530 --> 00:18:59.200
laid out a high level
Cloud migration roadmap.

00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:01.450
We started with a
focus on governance,

00:19:01.450 --> 00:19:04.270
whereby we set out
to establish controls

00:19:04.270 --> 00:19:08.290
around product ownership,
cost responsibility, roles

00:19:08.290 --> 00:19:11.770
and budgets, and
resource management,

00:19:11.770 --> 00:19:14.950
including folder
organization, project naming

00:19:14.950 --> 00:19:20.770
conventions, and required
project labels and SKU labels.

00:19:20.770 --> 00:19:22.480
From that foundation,
we could focus

00:19:22.480 --> 00:19:25.390
on cost visibility
and ultimately,

00:19:25.390 --> 00:19:27.053
on cost optimization.

00:19:30.300 --> 00:19:32.793
Let's first talk
about cost visibility.

00:19:36.660 --> 00:19:38.580
Our cost visibility
efforts have helped

00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:41.580
us understand what we
were spending money on,

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:44.250
sliced on a variety
of dimensions.

00:19:44.250 --> 00:19:48.810
For example, by projects,
by service, by region,

00:19:48.810 --> 00:19:53.220
and by label, to name
just a few dimensions.

00:19:53.220 --> 00:19:56.190
In addition to aggregate
cost visibility,

00:19:56.190 --> 00:20:00.300
we were focusing on
per unit cost metrics.

00:20:00.300 --> 00:20:04.560
Also, we prioritized cost
visibility around usage type,

00:20:04.560 --> 00:20:08.880
namely preemptible, on-demand,
and committed to use.

00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:11.460
And we turned on
GKE usage metering

00:20:11.460 --> 00:20:16.380
to measure our Kubernetes costs
by app, by cluster, and region.

00:20:20.210 --> 00:20:24.500
So how did we set up our
cost reporting framework?

00:20:24.500 --> 00:20:27.080
For some GCP customers,
the GCP console

00:20:27.080 --> 00:20:30.320
may provide a good
start to cost reporting.

00:20:30.320 --> 00:20:33.590
Early on, we turned on
Billing Data Export, which

00:20:33.590 --> 00:20:35.750
is highly recommended,
and started

00:20:35.750 --> 00:20:38.750
to integrate various data
sources into our recording

00:20:38.750 --> 00:20:40.530
framework.

00:20:40.530 --> 00:20:43.440
We used Data Studio and
BigQuery for the majority

00:20:43.440 --> 00:20:46.230
of our cost reporting.

00:20:46.230 --> 00:20:49.400
One of our key metrics
is how many ad requests

00:20:49.400 --> 00:20:51.260
do we receive each day?

00:20:51.260 --> 00:20:54.720
And what does it cost
us to process each one?

00:20:54.720 --> 00:20:58.980
We did this by integrating our
GCP billing data with our OpenX

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:00.730
performance data.

00:21:00.730 --> 00:21:04.110
This enabled us to get insights
into per unit costs as well

00:21:04.110 --> 00:21:07.120
as gross margin performance.

00:21:07.120 --> 00:21:09.340
In terms of frequency
of reporting,

00:21:09.340 --> 00:21:11.740
we look at cost reporting
daily, and we send out

00:21:11.740 --> 00:21:14.560
a cost summary email each
day to various teams.

00:21:17.750 --> 00:21:22.100
Now that I've touched on how we
set things up from a governance

00:21:22.100 --> 00:21:25.010
cost visibility and
reporting standpoint,

00:21:25.010 --> 00:21:27.826
we will turn our attention
to cost optimization.

00:21:30.950 --> 00:21:33.830
This grid represents
various cost optimizations

00:21:33.830 --> 00:21:36.230
in terms of
complexity of the work

00:21:36.230 --> 00:21:40.020
relative to the expected
cost saving benefits.

00:21:40.020 --> 00:21:42.240
Also, we have organized
the optimizations

00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:46.060
into four buckets:
usage optimization,

00:21:46.060 --> 00:21:52.090
pricing efficiency, waste
reduction, and refactoring.

00:21:52.090 --> 00:21:54.200
We are working on or
have completed work

00:21:54.200 --> 00:21:56.440
on all of the above.

00:21:56.440 --> 00:21:59.980
Overall, we have completed more
than 30 significant Cloud cost

00:21:59.980 --> 00:22:01.810
optimizations.

00:22:01.810 --> 00:22:04.060
I'll share some examples
in the following slides.

00:22:08.130 --> 00:22:11.280
First I'll share two examples
of usage optimizations.

00:22:14.590 --> 00:22:16.600
The first usage
optimization example

00:22:16.600 --> 00:22:19.510
is around saving
money on egress.

00:22:19.510 --> 00:22:21.340
We saw that we were
spending a lot of money

00:22:21.340 --> 00:22:25.030
on egress between zones, which
is called interzone egress

00:22:25.030 --> 00:22:27.040
in our regional clusters.

00:22:27.040 --> 00:22:29.850
See the orange
bars on this chart.

00:22:29.850 --> 00:22:32.820
By shifting to multi
zonal clusters,

00:22:32.820 --> 00:22:36.180
we were able to reduce
interzone egress costs to 0

00:22:36.180 --> 00:22:37.995
without impacting
other egress costs.

00:22:41.650 --> 00:22:44.560
The second usage
optimization example

00:22:44.560 --> 00:22:46.840
is around changing
the machine shape

00:22:46.840 --> 00:22:51.140
to better match
utilization of CPU and RAM.

00:22:51.140 --> 00:22:53.180
By evaluating
resource utilization

00:22:53.180 --> 00:22:55.760
within a specific
project, we found

00:22:55.760 --> 00:22:57.980
that we were fully
utilizing memory,

00:22:57.980 --> 00:23:00.560
but under utilizing CPU.

00:23:00.560 --> 00:23:02.880
See the purple bars.

00:23:02.880 --> 00:23:04.920
By changing the
machine shape, we

00:23:04.920 --> 00:23:08.550
were able to reduce costs
by 25% within this project.

00:23:12.110 --> 00:23:16.040
The last two examples were
about usage optimization.

00:23:16.040 --> 00:23:19.880
In the next few examples,
we'll cover pricing efficiency,

00:23:19.880 --> 00:23:22.340
finding the best price
solution for your work.

00:23:25.990 --> 00:23:28.250
The first pricing
efficiency example,

00:23:28.250 --> 00:23:30.410
and the most
important, is around

00:23:30.410 --> 00:23:34.610
leveraging discounted compute
rates for preemptible machines

00:23:34.610 --> 00:23:37.840
and implementing
committed use discounts.

00:23:37.840 --> 00:23:41.890
The red line represents
on-demand compute VCPUs.

00:23:41.890 --> 00:23:45.940
You can see a nice drop off in
our on-demand usage over time.

00:23:45.940 --> 00:23:48.760
Running more services
on preemptible machines,

00:23:48.760 --> 00:23:53.700
the gold line, had a very
dramatic cost reduction.

00:23:53.700 --> 00:23:55.370
Also note that some
refactoring may

00:23:55.370 --> 00:23:58.550
be required to run preemptible.

00:23:58.550 --> 00:24:00.720
The blue line
represents our increased

00:24:00.720 --> 00:24:02.700
three-year commitment.

00:24:02.700 --> 00:24:06.750
This change also led to a
significant cost reduction.

00:24:06.750 --> 00:24:10.100
The combination of preemptible
machines and committed use

00:24:10.100 --> 00:24:12.165
discounts dramatically
reduced our costs.

00:24:16.170 --> 00:24:18.240
The second pricing
efficiency example

00:24:18.240 --> 00:24:21.810
is around flat rate
pricing for BigQuery.

00:24:21.810 --> 00:24:24.690
We are a heavy user of
BigQuery, both for storage

00:24:24.690 --> 00:24:26.890
and for querying.

00:24:26.890 --> 00:24:30.660
We found that our on-demand
analysis costs were very spiky,

00:24:30.660 --> 00:24:34.420
and we had days where we
spent way more than expected.

00:24:34.420 --> 00:24:36.880
By committing to
flat rate pricing,

00:24:36.880 --> 00:24:39.820
we were able to better
control our costs.

00:24:39.820 --> 00:24:42.070
The downside is that some
queries may take longer

00:24:42.070 --> 00:24:47.410
to run if capacity demands
exceeded committed capacity.

00:24:47.410 --> 00:24:49.510
In our case, our
queries were primarily

00:24:49.510 --> 00:24:51.650
for analysis purposes.

00:24:51.650 --> 00:24:54.780
So we refined making a
performance trade off.

00:24:54.780 --> 00:24:58.240
As a result, our
on-demand cost dropped,

00:24:58.240 --> 00:25:00.240
and we attained a more
stable cost for BigQuery.

00:25:04.390 --> 00:25:07.570
The third and final
pricing efficiency example

00:25:07.570 --> 00:25:10.450
is around reducing
storage costs.

00:25:10.450 --> 00:25:13.750
By choosing the most cost
effective storage class,

00:25:13.750 --> 00:25:17.290
we reduced storage
costs by 50% by shifting

00:25:17.290 --> 00:25:19.990
from regional storage,
the blue bars,

00:25:19.990 --> 00:25:24.570
to a blend of coldline
and nearline storage.

00:25:24.570 --> 00:25:28.260
Also, while we are on
the subject of storage,

00:25:28.260 --> 00:25:31.960
we've done a lot of work with
data retention policy changes,

00:25:31.960 --> 00:25:33.890
a form of waste reduction.

00:25:33.890 --> 00:25:36.045
And these have also had
a cost saving impact.

00:25:38.850 --> 00:25:41.790
So what kind of cost impact did
these and other optimizations

00:25:41.790 --> 00:25:42.290
have?

00:25:45.620 --> 00:25:48.470
I mentioned earlier that one
of our key areas of focus

00:25:48.470 --> 00:25:51.500
was on optimizing
our per unit costs.

00:25:51.500 --> 00:25:53.480
One important cost
metric that we track

00:25:53.480 --> 00:25:55.930
is cost per ad request.

00:25:55.930 --> 00:26:00.240
This chart shows our cost
per ad request over time.

00:26:00.240 --> 00:26:03.660
Through a combination of
usage optimization, pricing

00:26:03.660 --> 00:26:08.520
efficiency, waste reduction, and
a fair amount of refactoring,

00:26:08.520 --> 00:26:12.300
we have reduced our per
unit costs by more than 60%

00:26:12.300 --> 00:26:14.970
since September of last year.

00:26:14.970 --> 00:26:17.480
This is all during a time
of increased ad unit growth.

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:23.210
Now we'll wrap up with
some lessons learned.

00:26:26.420 --> 00:26:28.760
Here are some key takeaways
from our experience

00:26:28.760 --> 00:26:32.010
optimizing costs in GCP.

00:26:32.010 --> 00:26:36.060
Cost management and optimization
is not a one person job.

00:26:36.060 --> 00:26:38.310
Your success will
depend on good teamwork

00:26:38.310 --> 00:26:41.540
across the organization.

00:26:41.540 --> 00:26:44.240
It might be boring
work, but an early focus

00:26:44.240 --> 00:26:48.400
on governance, resource
management, and controls

00:26:48.400 --> 00:26:50.500
will help pave the way
to success in the Cloud.

00:26:53.070 --> 00:26:54.780
Early on, we had
a few days of pain

00:26:54.780 --> 00:26:57.750
with costs that
spiraled out of control.

00:26:57.750 --> 00:27:00.390
At the top of our
do-overs list, would

00:27:00.390 --> 00:27:02.610
be to implement
project budgeting

00:27:02.610 --> 00:27:07.100
and or cost spike detection
even earlier than we did.

00:27:07.100 --> 00:27:10.780
Where possible, utilize
preemptible machines.

00:27:10.780 --> 00:27:13.730
And for long term
consistent usage

00:27:13.730 --> 00:27:15.890
that would otherwise
be on-demand,

00:27:15.890 --> 00:27:20.750
it may be worth it to make a one
year or three year commitment.

00:27:20.750 --> 00:27:22.880
Finally, there are
many different ways

00:27:22.880 --> 00:27:25.730
to optimize costs in the Cloud.

00:27:25.730 --> 00:27:29.090
Cost optimizations vary with
level of effort and the cost

00:27:29.090 --> 00:27:30.780
saving impact.

00:27:30.780 --> 00:27:33.560
So prioritize accordingly.

00:27:33.560 --> 00:27:36.130
Now I'll hand it back to Pathik.

00:27:36.130 --> 00:27:36.640
Thank you.

00:27:40.232 --> 00:27:41.940
PATHIK SHARMA: Thanks
Scott for taking us

00:27:41.940 --> 00:27:45.100
through that transformative
journey of OpenX.

00:27:45.100 --> 00:27:47.040
It doesn't matter
if you are a startup

00:27:47.040 --> 00:27:49.380
or 100-year-old enterprise.

00:27:49.380 --> 00:27:52.720
Process and optimization
tips shared in this session,

00:27:52.720 --> 00:27:57.690
and by Scott, can be leveraged
across all types of customers.

00:27:57.690 --> 00:28:00.840
If you still need help, engage
Google Cloud Professional

00:28:00.840 --> 00:28:05.650
Services to jump start your
cost performance journey.

